// Practice Bank 12 — CCAR-P Module 2 Drill: Enterprise Integration & Production (66 items)
// Written from the Anthropic Partner Academy prep course "Enterprise Integration & Production" (Module 2): evals, POC to production, sizing and feasibility, integration patterns, A/B testing and observability.
// The course says its pricing, caching and model details change; verify against platform.claude.com. Domains use ids 11–17 (see js/tests.js). Correct option(s) first; the app shuffles.
window.EXAMS = window.EXAMS || [];
window.EXAMS.push({
  id: 12,
  title: "CCAR-P Module 2 Drill: Enterprise Integration & Production",
  questions: [
{d:14,q:"A team plans to build the contract-review assistant first and write its evals during QA. According to the course, why does the order matter, and what should change?",
o:["Write the eval suite before production code: it forces success to be stated measurably, exposes design assumptions while changes are cheap, and creates a gate for later changes",
"Write evals after the first working version, because a running system shows which behaviors are worth measuring and avoids wasted effort on tests for features that never ship",
"Write evals in parallel with the build but keep them informal until launch, because rigid pass criteria set too early lock the team into thresholds that the prototype cannot meet",
"Write evals once, before launch, from the contracts the team knows best, because familiar documents make expected outputs easy to label and the suite quick to complete"],
a:[0],e:"An eval suite belongs at the start of the build, not at the end as a QA step. If you cannot write an eval for a behavior, you cannot verify it, so every change to the system is unverifiable. Building evals first forces measurable success criteria, exposes assumptions early, and gives you a gate for any model swap, prompt change or retrieval change."},
{d:14,q:"Which order matches the course's five-stage eval workflow?",
o:["Define the task and pass criteria; build the golden dataset; run automated checks; score with a judge; interpret per-category results and act",
"Build the golden dataset; define the task and pass criteria; score with a judge; run automated checks; interpret the overall mean and act on it",
"Define the task and pass criteria; score with a judge; run automated checks; build the golden dataset; interpret per-category results and act",
"Run automated checks; define the task; build the golden dataset; score with a human reviewer; interpret the overall score and release if it rose"],
a:[0],e:"The stages are: 1 define the task (measurable behavior plus the test prompt), 2 build the golden dataset (including edge cases and counterexamples), 3 run automated checks (fast, cheap, unambiguous), 4 score with a judge for behaviors needing interpretation, 5 interpret and act, using the per-category breakdown, because a higher mean can hide worse edge-case performance."},
{d:14,q:"Which grading ladder does the course prescribe?",
o:["Code-based grading wherever the behavior allows it, an LLM judge when interpretation is needed, and human grading only as the last resort",
"An LLM judge for everything first, because it covers every behavior, then code-based checks for cost savings, and human review for any disputes",
"Human grading first to establish quality, then an LLM judge to scale it, and code-based checks only for trivial formatting that nobody disputes",
"Code-based grading for tone and style, an LLM judge for schema and format, and human grading for high-volume regression runs on each change"],
a:[0],e:"Reach for the cheapest reliable method first and climb only when the behavior demands it. Code checks run in milliseconds, cost almost nothing and never drift; judges handle interpretation (tone, reasoning quality, edge-case appropriateness); humans are slow and expensive, so reserve them for high-stakes or novel behavior and for calibrating judges."},
{d:14,q:"Match each behavior to the cheapest reliable eval type: (1) output is valid JSON matching a schema, (2) the reply's tone fits the brand, (3) a safety-critical edge case in a new capability with no rubric.",
o:["(1) code-based, (2) model-based judge, (3) human review",
"(1) model-based judge, (2) code-based, (3) human review",
"(1) code-based, (2) human review, (3) model-based judge",
"(1) human review, (2) model-based judge, (3) code-based"],
a:[0],e:"Schema validity is unambiguous, so a deterministic check is enough. Tone needs interpretation, which is what a rubric-driven judge does at scale. Novel, high-stakes behavior without an established rubric is where neither code nor a judge can be trusted yet, so humans grade it."},
{d:14,q:"A team's LLM judge returns confident scores, and nobody has compared it with people. What does the course say, and what should they do?",
o:["An uncalibrated judge is worse than no automated grade because it looks trustworthy; run it against human-labeled outputs and confirm agreement is high enough to rely on",
"The judge can be trusted because it receives the rubric, so the team should only spot-check its scores after launch if complaints appear about specific answers in practice",
"The judge should be replaced by a second judge from the same model family, because two scores that agree with each other are good evidence that the grades are right overall",
"The judge should be retired, because any automated grading of interpretive behavior is unreliable and the course reserves all such grading for human reviewers"],
a:[0],e:"Judge calibration is the step many teams skip. A judge is itself a system that can be wrong. Make it rigorous with detailed rubrics, constrained verdicts (a small fixed label set), reasoning alongside the score, calibration against human-labeled examples, and a different model than the one being evaluated, to avoid self-preference."},
{d:14,q:"A business requirement says “summarize claims accurately.” How should it become an eval criterion, according to the course?",
o:["Name the behavior (extract filer, claim number, incident date, amount), set thresholds from the business requirement, list failure modes, and include adversarial inputs",
"Keep the wording and score summaries on a one-to-five scale, setting the passing threshold at whatever accuracy the first prototype achieves on a sample of claims overall",
"Define accuracy as agreement with an expert's summary and set the threshold at 100% for all fields, then add edge cases only when failures appear in production",
"Break the requirement into tone, length and completeness, score each with a judge, and take the threshold from the vendor's published benchmark for summarization"],
a:[0],e:"A vague requirement produces a vague eval. Turn it into specific behaviors, set pass thresholds that come from the business requirement (not from what the prototype happens to reach), identify failure modes (fake claim number, missing date, wrong-claim value) as dataset categories, and include missing fields, handwriting and non-standard layouts."},
{d:14,q:"A golden dataset contains only clean, typical documents. What is the problem, per the course?",
o:["Scores will not predict production performance; the dataset must include the edge cases, counterexamples and adversarial inputs the system will meet",
"Clean data makes scores artificially low, so the team should lower the thresholds until typical documents pass and add hard cases in a later project phase",
"Clean data is ideal for a first baseline, so the dataset should stay as it is until the system reaches production and real failures can be added then",
"The dataset is fine if it is large, because volume of clean examples smooths out variation and makes the mean score a reliable predictor of quality"],
a:[0],e:"If the dataset is not representative, the scores are not meaningful. The course's contract-review postmortem built its suite from ten convenient contracts, so it never covered non-standard obligation structures, and the regression arrived two weeks after launch."},
{d:14,q:"Which changes should run through the eval suite before reaching production?",
o:["Every change: a model swap, a prompt revision, a context strategy change or a retrieval configuration update, because evals are the only reliable way to know it helped",
"Model swaps only, because prompts and retrieval settings can be adjusted freely and rolled back quickly if users report a problem after the change goes live in practice",
"Model swaps and prompt revisions, because context strategy and retrieval changes affect cost and latency but cannot change output quality in a measurable way by default",
"Only changes that engineers believe are risky, because running the suite on every small edit costs more in judge calls than it saves in avoided regressions"],
a:[0],e:"Evals are the gating mechanism for change. An out-of-date suite is dangerous: it gives false confidence, because the checks measure behavior that no longer exists. The highest-risk moment for a regression is when evals are present but out of date."},
{d:14,q:"A team revised its summarization prompt but did not update the eval suite, which kept passing. Two days later, multi-clause legal sentences were being truncated in production. What is the root cause?",
o:["The eval set predated the prompt change and did not match the behavior that changed, so passing it said nothing about the new behavior at all",
"The judge model was too lenient, so replacing it with a stricter judge would have flagged the truncation before the prompt change reached production",
"The prompt change was too large, so it should have been released in smaller steps with a manual spot-check of ten documents after each one",
"The eval set was too small, so doubling the number of documents of the same kind would have caught the truncation on the very first run after release"],
a:[0],e:"Keep the eval set current with the system it measures and run evals before every change. A suite that still passes only because the golden dataset reflects the old prompt's expected outputs is measuring a different system than the one shipping."},
{d:14,q:"Why does the course treat multi-turn evals as a separate category?",
o:["The scored unit is a whole conversation, so it needs its own golden dataset of full transcripts with known good responses at each turn, including follow-ups and topic shifts",
"Multi-turn behavior is just single-turn behavior repeated, so a larger single-turn dataset covers it as long as it includes prompts that mention earlier answers",
"Multi-turn evals are only needed for voice products, because text conversations are short enough that single-turn scores already predict how long conversations behave",
"Multi-turn evals replace single-turn evals once a system is in production, because live conversations show quality problems that offline single-turn tests never reveal overall"],
a:[0],e:"A single-turn set does not show whether the system keeps context straight across turns, answers follow-ups without inventing earlier details, or holds quality as conversations lengthen. Multi-turn evals score sequences of exchanges, so their dataset is full conversation transcripts."},
{d:14,q:"A team argues for a tiny, painstakingly hand-graded eval set over a large automated one. What does the course advise?",
o:["Favor volume over perfection: many automatically gradable cases catch more regressions than a few manual ones, and they can run on every change",
"Favor the small hand-graded set, because careful grading of a few cases is more reliable than automated checks, which drift and cannot be trusted at scale",
"Favor the small set until launch, then expand it, because the cost of judge calls grows with dataset size and should be limited during development",
"Favor whichever is cheaper this quarter, because dataset size is a budget decision and the course sets no preference between broad and narrow coverage"],
a:[0],e:"Broad, cheap coverage catches more regressions than a small painstaking set and can run on every change. Cost is a secondary constraint: the bigger risk is under-evaluating, because a production-breaking change that slips through an undersized suite costs far more than extra API calls."},
{d:14,q:"Which four dimensions does the course say a POC hides but production exposes?",
o:["Cost, latency, reliability and failure modes",
"Accuracy, recall, precision and fluency of outputs",
"Cost, accuracy, security and user interface quality",
"Latency, throughput, accuracy and model version drift"],
a:[0],e:"A POC runs at low volume on clean inputs with a patient user. Cost looks negligible, single-request latency hides p95 under load, there are no retries or fallbacks, and the inputs the developer expects hide the failure modes production will raise."},
{d:14,q:"A cost model used the average token count of the sample documents. After launch the bill is far higher, and long documents in the tail account for 80% of spend. What should the model have done?",
o:["Model the token distribution rather than the average, since a tail of long requests can consume a disproportionate share of the total cost",
"Use the median token count instead of the mean, since the median represents the typical request and removes the effect of unusual documents",
"Add a fixed 20% safety margin to the average cost, since a margin of that size covers the variation between short and long requests",
"Cap input length at the average and reject longer documents, since long requests are rare enough that rejecting them has little business impact"],
a:[0],e:"Token distributions are often skewed. A cost model based on average usage can underestimate cost by a factor of two or three. Also run a sensitivity analysis: what happens if volume doubles or the distribution shifts toward the tail."},
{d:14,q:"Why does the course prefer p95 over the median as a latency design target?",
o:["Median reflects the middle of the pack, while SLA breaches come from the slowest requests; p95 is the value below which 95% of requests complete",
"p95 is easier to compute than the median for streaming systems, and it is the figure that most cloud providers publish in their service level agreements",
"The median hides concurrency effects only when the system is idle, so p95 matters for demos but not for production workloads under real concurrent load",
"p95 averages the five slowest requests, which makes it a more stable number than the median when traffic is low and individual requests vary widely"],
a:[0],e:"A demo runs one request at a time, so median under a single request hides latency under concurrent load. Only the slowest 5% of requests fall above p95, which is where user abandonment and SLA breaches concentrate."},
{d:14,q:"Which statement about prompt caching in cost and latency modeling matches the course?",
o:["Savings scale with prefix length and reuse, writes cost more than standard input, the default TTL is five minutes, and cached content creates a consistency window",
"Savings are fixed per request regardless of prefix length, writes cost the same as standard input, and cached content is refreshed automatically on every call overall",
"Caching only helps output tokens, requires no markers in the request, and works best when the prompt starts with the per-request content before the fixed part",
"Caching only helps output tokens, requires no markers in the request, and works best when the prompt starts with the per-request content before the fixed part overall"],
a:[0],e:"Caching needs explicit cache_control markers. Cache reads are charged at a fraction of the input rate, so a long stable prefix reused often saves the most; workloads with request frequency below the TTL will not realize consistent savings. If cached content must reflect live state, caching can violate the use case's consistency requirements."},
{d:13,q:"Where does the course say each reliability control belongs?",
o:["Retries close to the API call, circuit breakers at the service boundary, and fallback chains in the orchestration layer",
"Retries in the orchestration layer, circuit breakers close to the API call, and fallback chains at the service boundary overall",
"All three in the orchestration layer, so that one component owns every failure decision and the call stack stays simple",
"Retries at the service boundary, circuit breakers in the orchestration layer, and fallback chains close to the API call"],
a:[0],e:"Controls must sit at the right stage to be effective: placing them in the wrong layer protects the wrong part of the system and leaves the right part exposed."},
{d:13,q:"An endpoint returns a 429 rate-limit error during a traffic spike. Which handling does the course describe?",
o:["Retry with exponential backoff and a capped number of attempts and total wait, so a brief hiccup does not become a prolonged outage",
"Retry immediately and repeatedly until the call succeeds, because the fastest recovery from a transient error is an instant second attempt",
"Retry at a fixed one-second interval with no cap, because a predictable schedule makes behavior easier to reason about during an incident",
"Do not retry rate-limit errors and return an error to the user, because retries during a spike add load that makes the limit worse"],
a:[0],e:"Transient errors (429, timeout, 5xx) call for progressively longer delays between attempts, with the maximum attempts and total wait set by how much delay the use case tolerates. This prevents a flood of retries from turning a brief hiccup into an outage."},
{d:13,q:"What does a circuit breaker do, and what is its value?",
o:["It tracks a dependency's error rate and trips past a threshold so requests fail immediately, keeping one degraded dependency from taking down the system",
"It retries failed requests on a schedule with growing delays, so that transient errors on a dependency are absorbed before any user sees a failure overall",
"It routes requests to an alternative model tier when the primary is unavailable, so the user always receives some answer from the workflow in the meantime",
"It limits the number of tool calls an agent can make in one turn, so that a runaway loop cannot consume the entire token budget for a single request"],
a:[0],e:"Once tripped, requests fail immediately instead of waiting for a timeout. Retries handle transient errors, fallback chains reroute to an alternative, and turn or tool-call limits belong to agent budgeting. Fallback behavior should be tested as part of your eval suite."},
{d:13,q:"At peak load on day three, the endpoint returned a 529 and the whole document-triage workflow went down. What was missing, and what is the underlying lesson?",
o:["Fallback handling that had never been tested; a POC establishes capability but not cost, input distribution or what happens when a dependency fails",
"A larger rate limit; the team should have requested higher quotas before launch so that peak traffic could not trigger an error at the endpoint overall",
"A faster model for the workflow; the endpoint was overloaded because the chosen tier was too slow, so moving to a smaller tier would have avoided the error",
"A user-facing retry button; the workflow was correct, and letting users resubmit failed items would have kept the system available until recovery overall"],
a:[0],e:"A POC has no retries, fallbacks or circuit breakers; when it fails the developer refreshes. Without fallback handling, any transient API failure takes the whole user-facing workflow down instead of degrading gracefully. Failure cases must be designed in and tested separately."},
{d:13,q:"Which pairing of architecture and its first-breaking failure mode, with the course's mitigation, is correct?",
o:["Agent: unbounded tool use and growing context; set per-turn token budgets, tool-call limits and stopping criteria, and eval stopping behavior",
"RAG: unbounded tool use and growing context; set per-turn token budgets, tool-call limits and stopping criteria, and eval stopping behavior",
"Agent: retrieval quality drift after index changes; monitor precision and recall, separate live-state queries from static knowledge queries",
"Document pipeline: failure boundaries blur between workers; define recoverable and unrecoverable boundaries and share one trace ID everywhere"],
a:[0],e:"Agent: unbounded tool use and growing context. RAG: retrieval quality drift. Document processing pipeline (evaluator-optimizer): no exception path for low-confidence extractions, so add confidence scoring and route to human review. Orchestrator-workers: blurred failure boundaries, fragmented traces and silent dropped subagents."},
{d:14,q:"A document-processing pipeline sends every document through the same flow, so edge cases fail at the same rate clean documents succeed. What does the course recommend?",
o:["Add confidence scoring to the extraction step and route low-confidence extractions to a human review queue instead of downstream processing",
"Add a second extraction pass for every document and accept the answer when the two passes agree, discarding documents where they differ",
"Increase the model tier for all documents so edge cases are handled by a more capable model, and drop the human review queue to save cost",
"Remove edge-case documents from the eval set so scores reflect typical performance, and handle exceptions manually outside the pipeline"],
a:[0],e:"A pipeline with no exception path produces wrong outputs on edge cases at the same rate it produces correct outputs on clean documents. Include difficult documents in the eval set as well."},
{d:13,q:"What kind of control is model version pinning, and what should accompany it?",
o:["An operational discipline that applies to every architecture: pin versions in configuration, monitor deprecations, and keep a version-update runbook ready",
"An architecture choice that applies only to agents, because workflows and RAG systems are insensitive to which model version serves their requests",
"A one-time launch task that is complete when the model identifier is set, since versions never change behavior until the team chooses to update them",
"A cost control that applies to batch workloads, because pinned versions receive the discounted batch rate while unpinned requests pay the standard rate"],
a:[0],e:"Pinning is operational discipline, not architecture. Without it, a version can roll forward and change behavior with no re-validation checkpoint, which is the governance gap in Module 1's agent retrospective."},
{d:11,q:"A POC ran 10–50 requests per day, and the team shows the partner the raw demo bill. What does the course say?",
o:["A POC bill informs a production estimate only when scaled to expected volume with error bounds; raw demo costs shown without extrapolation are the risk",
"A POC bill is useless for estimating production cost, so the team should refuse to estimate until the system has been live for a full billing cycle",
"A POC bill scales linearly with volume, so multiplying the demo cost by the ratio of production to demo requests gives a reliable production estimate",
"A POC bill is accurate if the demo used the production model tier, since tier is the only input that changes the price of a request at larger volume"],
a:[0],e:"A POC is built to prove capability. It also gives a first signal on whether the system moves the business metric; capture that deliberately. A cost profile that fits the budget on a system that does not improve the metric that matters is still a failed deployment."},
{d:11,q:"Which four inputs drive the course's use-case sizing model?",
o:["Call volume, token budget per request, model tier, and sensitivity parameters",
"Call volume, response accuracy, model tier, and the number of evaluation runs",
"Token budget per request, cache hit rate, number of users, and the size of the team",
"Model tier, latency target, budget ceiling, and the date the partner expects launch"],
a:[0],e:"Call volume comes from the business owner, not developer intuition. The token budget has input and output components, modeled as a distribution. Monthly cost multiplies volume by input and output tokens at their separate rates; then run sensitivity analysis (volume doubles, tail grows)."},
{d:11,q:"A workload is latency-tolerant and large. How does the course say to treat the Batch API in cost modeling, and what must be checked for regulated data?",
o:["Model it as an alternative at roughly 50% off for asynchronous work, and verify the BAA and compliance configuration cover batch before routing PHI",
"Model it as a replacement for every workload, since it is cheaper, and assume that any BAA covering the synchronous API covers batch processing",
"Avoid it for cost modeling, since its discount applies only to output tokens, and use caching instead because the two features cannot be combined at all",
"Model it only for workloads under a few hundred requests, since larger batches lose the discount, and skip compliance checks because batch results are not stored"],
a:[0],e:"Batch API offers a price reduction relative to standard pricing and supports large batches, but the course says to confirm current rates on the pricing page. For regulated workloads, coverage under the partner's BAA and compliance configuration must be verified before routing governed data through it."},
{d:11,q:"Which three feasibility verdicts does the course define, and what must accompany a verdict of “feasible with constraints”?",
o:["Feasible as scoped, feasible with constraints, and not feasible; constraints must be documented explicitly with the failure mode for each violated one",
"Feasible, risky, and not feasible; the risks must be listed in the project plan and re-reviewed at the first retrospective after the launch",
"Feasible as scoped, partially feasible, and not feasible; partial verdicts require a smaller pilot and an executive sign-off before any build begins",
"Feasible now, feasible later, and not feasible; deferred verdicts are revisited each quarter when new model releases change the capability assessment"],
a:[0],e:"A feasible-with-constraints verdict that is not documented becomes an infeasible system when constraints are violated in production. A not-feasible verdict is a correct assessment that saves the engagement from a more expensive failure, and should name the disqualifying constraint and any scope reduction that would change it."},
{d:11,q:"What is the course's four-step scoping sequence?",
o:["Requirement to capability list; capability list to architecture sketch; sketch to boundary conditions; boundary conditions into the statement of work",
"Requirement to architecture sketch; sketch to cost model; cost model to a working prototype; prototype results into the statement of work for the team",
"Capability demo to partner approval; approval to architecture sketch; sketch to estimate; estimate into the statement of work as written from the start",
"Requirement to eval suite; eval suite to model choice; model choice to integration plan; the plan into the statement of work as agreed with the sponsor"],
a:[0],e:"Skipping any step produces a commitment that will not survive the next conversation with the business owner. Example boundary condition: an architecture that works for documents up to 20 pages but fails for longer ones must document that limit and put it in the SOW."},
{d:11,q:"During discovery the partner says: 800 contracts a day, some 300 pages, results in under 30 seconds. The architect had already said the build was feasible. What does the course conclude?",
o:["The verdict preceded the volume, input-size and latency constraints; at 800 a day volume pressures cost more than latency, and context window size is a tier decision",
"The verdict stands, because 800 requests a day requires parallel processing to meet a 30-second target and any model can handle a 300-page contract without chunking",
"The project is not feasible, because 300-page contracts exceed every model's context window and the 30-second target cannot be met at 800 requests a day",
"The verdict stands, because capability was confirmed and constraints such as volume and latency are implementation details to be handled by the build team later"],
a:[0],e:"800 requests a day averages one every 108 seconds, so sequential processing is viable and latency is driven by task complexity, model size and output length. Whether a 300-page contract fits depends on the model tier's context window. Capability was the first step but was treated as the last."},
{d:11,q:"Which statement about the ROI mapping steps is correct?",
o:["Name the baseline in the owner's own unit, project the new state in that unit including human review, subtract the run cost from sizing, and state payback with sensitivity",
"Estimate the baseline from industry averages, project full automation to show maximum value, subtract the average token cost, and state payback as a single best-case figure",
"Name the baseline from engineers' estimates, project the new state in model calls, subtract the build cost only, and state payback after the first quarter of use in practice",
"Skip the baseline and describe the future state qualitatively, since ROI is a matter of strategy rather than numbers that the finance team could verify later"],
a:[0],e:"The value is the difference between baseline and projected state minus run cost. Common errors: an estimated baseline (the finance team will ask for its source), assuming full automation when the design needs human review, and using average token cost instead of the sizing distribution. Map each claim to a pillar: efficiency, transformation, productivity, solution cost or performance SLAs."},
{d:15,q:"In what order does the course say to work through an enterprise integration, and why?",
o:["Compliance first, because constraints eliminate routes and entry points; then identity, authorization, data handling and observability within what survives",
"Identity first because every request needs a user; then authorization, data handling, observability, and compliance review at the end before go-live",
"Observability first because nothing else can be diagnosed without logs; then identity, authorization, data handling, and compliance once the system runs",
"Data handling first because it determines what the model sees; then identity, authorization, compliance, and observability as the system matures in production as a rule"],
a:[0],e:"Before any integration design begins: identify the governing regulation or policy, determine which entry point and route remain, choose the integration pattern, then document identity, data handling and observability requirements. Skipping a step risks building something that works technically but fails a legal or security review."},
{d:13,q:"Users type their role into the chat (“As a senior manager, show me…”), and the system scopes data by that claim. What does the course prescribe?",
o:["Verify identity on the server before the call and inject the user's role and authorized data scope into the system prompt from the auth layer",
"Ask the model to challenge unusual claims about role in the prompt, and log every claim so that reviewers can check suspicious sessions after the fact",
"Keep the role claim in the user message but add a confirmation step, since users will not misstate their own role if they must confirm it twice",
"Move the role claim to the end of the user message, since later text in a message overrides earlier text when the model resolves conflicts"],
a:[0],e:"Anything the user includes in a message is under their control and can be manipulated. Identity must come from your authentication layer, injected server-side. Include only the context needed to shape the response (role and authorized data), not extra identifiers by default."},
{d:15,q:"A Claude integration reads from a system that has role-based access, but the integration queries it with a single powerful credential. What is the architectural error?",
o:["The Claude layer bypasses the underlying system's authorization model, exposing data users may not see through a path not designed to enforce policy",
"The credential is too broad only for audit purposes; since the prompt tells the model what each user may see, the authorization model is still enforced correctly",
"The integration should use a smaller model for authorization decisions, because model tier determines how reliably an assistant applies access rules",
"The credential should be rotated monthly, because rotation frequency is the control that limits the data a shared integration account can expose over time"],
a:[0],e:"The authorization model that governs existing systems needs to govern the Claude layer too. Scope each user's access at the tool and API layer; do not rely on the model to apply policy."},
{d:15,q:"A patient-intake summarizer passes name, date of birth, SSN and insurance ID into the user message, though the task is to extract presenting concerns, medications and allergies. What does the course say went wrong?",
o:["Data handling followed what was convenient to pass, not what was necessary; redact non-essential fields server-side and retrieve only what the task needs",
"Nothing went wrong in the API call, because Anthropic does not retain content by default, so the application's own logs are the only place the fields could appear",
"The model tier was wrong; a larger model would have summarized the intake form accurately without needing the identifying fields to appear in the request at all",
"The prompt was too short; adding the patient's full chart to the context would have let the model decide for itself which identifying fields mattered most"],
a:[0],e:"The context window is not a data-governance boundary: whatever is passed is transmitted with the request, and the partner's application layer typically logs it in plaintext. Necessity is the filter: if a field is not required for the language task, it should not be in the context window. Reference identifiers are often enough."},
{d:13,q:"Which four things does the course say a production Claude system should log?",
o:["The request (model version, input tokens, prompt ID), the response (output tokens, latency, stop reason), the context (role, session, caching), and the outcome signal",
"The user's message, the model's reply, the user's identity, and the date, retained forever so any incident can be reconstructed from full text",
"Errors, timeouts, rate limits and restarts, since a model rarely fails in any way that standard infrastructure logging would not already capture",
"Cost per request, latency p50, daily active users and thumbs ratings, since these four metrics together describe quality and spending for stakeholders"],
a:[0],e:"A Claude system does not crash when something goes wrong; it produces a subtly wrong response, which standard logging does not catch. Security organizations increasingly treat observability as the precondition for enabling agents at all: an action taken but not logged cannot be allowed, so verify which agentic actions your chosen surfaces record."},
{d:15,q:"How does the course say to configure tools in an orchestrator-worker deployment?",
o:["Audit tools like permissions: keep only those essential to the task, record why others were removed, and scope each subagent's tool access to its own task",
"Give the orchestrator every tool and let subagents inherit them, since subagents then never fail for lack of a capability when the orchestrator delegates overall",
"Give every subagent the same broad tool set, since uniform access makes behavior easier to reason about and simplifies the trust model across the system overall",
"Add tools freely during development and remove unused ones after launch, once usage logs show which tools the agents actually called in production use"],
a:[0],e:"Every tool connected to a Claude system is an attack surface and a cost. Establish the trust hierarchy by scoping each subagent's tools to its task so it cannot reach tools its job does not require."},
{d:13,q:"A multi-tenant platform serves all customers through one shared API key. At peak the org-level rate limit trips. What is the problem and the fix?",
o:["The spike is visible but its source is not, so every tenant absorbs the impact; use separate API keys per tenant for attribution and isolation",
"The limit is too low, so the fix is to request a higher organization limit and keep the shared key, since attribution is a reporting problem for later",
"The tenants are sending requests too fast, so the fix is a client-side delay of one second between each tenant's requests across the platform",
"The key is too old, so the fix is monthly rotation, since a freshly issued key resets the rate limit window for the whole organization on that day"],
a:[0],e:"With a shared key you cannot attribute a rate-limit breach to the tenant that caused it. Separate keys per tenant are required for attribution and isolation in any production multi-tenant deployment."},
{d:14,q:"Which hypothesis meets the course's standard for an A/B test of a prompt change?",
o:["Replacing “summarize” with “extract the three most important action items” will raise task success rate by at least 5% without degrading latency p95",
"The new prompt is better than the old prompt for customer sessions, and the team will confirm this by looking at whichever metrics improve most",
"The new prompt will improve quality, as judged by the product manager after reading a selection of outputs from both versions this month",
"The new prompt will not make anything worse, and the test will end as soon as the treatment shows a higher success rate than the control"],
a:[0],e:"A usable hypothesis names the treatment, the primary metric and expected direction, the threshold for success, and any constraint on secondary metrics. Without it, any result can be read as a win."},
{d:14,q:"What does the course say about reading A/B results?",
o:["Significance is separate from importance: ask whether the effect justifies the operational overhead and whether any secondary metric, such as cost, degraded",
"A statistically significant result is a winner by definition, because significance means the effect is real and large enough to justify shipping it",
"A result is trustworthy once the treatment group has run longer than the control group, because extra time reduces the effect of the input distribution overall",
"Secondary metrics should be ignored once the primary metric wins, because tracking more than one metric makes it too easy to explain away a result"],
a:[0],e:"A change can be significant yet too small to justify shipping and maintaining; a prompt that lifts success while raising cost 30% may not be a net win. Also watch interaction effects between the treatment and rare input types, and align the test period with the input distribution."},
{d:14,q:"A team compared 50 sessions on a new prompt with 50 on the old: 68% versus 62%. They shipped it, and two weeks later success settled at 61%. Which issues does the course list?",
o:["The sample was too small to separate signal from noise, input distribution was not controlled, and the primary metric was chosen after seeing the result",
"The test ran for too short a time, the judge was too lenient, and the rollout was not gradual enough to catch the regression before it reached everyone overall",
"The control was too large relative to the treatment, the success metric was too coarse, and the prompt change should have been tested on a smaller model first",
"The metric moved the wrong way for latency, the sessions were not stratified by language, and the two groups should have been run on different weeks"],
a:[0],e:"A 6-point gap on a high-variance metric needs hundreds of sessions to reach significance. Treatment sessions happened to contain fewer edge cases. Picking the metric after the fact turns a test into a search for whatever moved. Calculate sample size from minimum detectable effect, baseline and confidence level before running."},
{d:14,q:"When does the course say shadow testing beats a live A/B test, and what is its cost?",
o:["When one bad output carries too much risk or traffic is too low for a live split; outputs are logged, not served, at the cost of real user-behavior signal",
"When traffic is very high and quick results are needed; it serves the new version to all users at once, at the cost of a longer rollback if problems appear",
"When the team lacks an offline rubric or golden answers; users score the shadow output themselves, at the cost of a slower feedback loop on the new version",
"When the change affects only cost and not quality; the new version replaces the old one silently, at the cost of losing the ability to compare latency"],
a:[0],e:"Shadow testing runs the new version in parallel on a copy of live requests, serves every user the current version, and scores the new outputs offline, so the decision comes before any user sees the change. In regulated industries it may be the only acceptable validation. The cost is losing downstream signals like whether users accepted the answer."},
{d:13,q:"Aggregate dashboards look healthy, yet a small fraction of requests consumes most of the budget and returns wrong answers. What does the course prescribe?",
o:["Separate per-request observability from aggregate dashboards: aggregates catch obvious failures, but per-request decomposition catches the non-obvious ones",
"Add more aggregate panels such as p99 latency and daily cost, because enough aggregate views will reveal any concentration of spend in a few requests at scale",
"Sample five percent of requests for manual review each week, because per-request tracing is too expensive for production systems at any real scale",
"Wait for the weekly cost report to show an anomaly, then investigate only the highest-cost day, since earlier requests will already be averaged out"],
a:[0],e:"Production observability needs request-level tracing (model, version, tokens, latency, stop reason, tool calls), metric aggregation, anomaly detection and change attribution. A cost spike above about 150% of the 7-day average or a p95 over the SLA deserves an alert; drift needs periodic distribution comparison."},
{d:14,q:"A metric moved after a release. Which three causes does the course tell you to distinguish, since each has a different fix?",
o:["Model drift (behavior changed on stable inputs), data drift (the inputs changed), and model update effects (a new version behaves differently)",
"Prompt drift (the prompt changed), user drift (the users changed), and network drift (latency changed), each fixed by reverting the most recent deployment",
"Training drift, retrieval drift and tokenizer drift, each fixed by re-embedding the corpus and re-running the original launch evaluation suite",
"Seasonal drift, pricing drift and regional drift, each fixed by adjusting the thresholds on the dashboards until the alerts stop firing for the team"],
a:[0],e:"Change attribution requires instrumentation that can tell these apart; mixing them up produces the wrong fix. Alongside it, classify failures: prompt failure (fix the prompt), hallucination (fix with grounding, not stronger instructions), model mismatch (fix with model selection gated by eval), orchestrator-workers failure (needs a trace spanning agents)."},
{d:14,q:"A model produces fluent, confident content that is not grounded in the input. Which failure class and fix does the course give?",
o:["Hallucination: ground the output through retrieval, tool use or verification; a stronger instruction will not resolve it on its own",
"Prompt failure: rewrite the instruction with more detail, because ambiguous wording is the usual cause of confident but ungrounded content",
"Model mismatch: move to a larger tier immediately, because ungrounded output means the chosen model lacks the capability for the task",
"Data drift: refresh the golden dataset with recent inputs, because ungrounded output follows from stale evaluation data in production"],
a:[0],e:"Prompt failure is an ambiguous or underspecified instruction (fix the prompt). Hallucination is confident content not grounded in the input or a reliable source (fix by grounding). Model mismatch is the wrong tier for the task or an un-reevaluated swap (fix by eval-gated model selection)."},
{d:16,q:"The people who funded the deployment read a KPI dashboard, not request traces. What does the course say the observability stack needs, and when?",
o:["A translation layer mapping technical metrics to the business metrics they drive (for example task success to first-contact resolution), built at design time",
"A separate executive report written by hand each quarter from the trace data, since business owners cannot interpret live technical metrics at all",
"A single combined health score that merges latency, cost and accuracy into one number, added after the first business review shows what leaders ask",
"Nothing extra, because business owners learn to read technical dashboards quickly, and a translation layer adds maintenance cost without much value"],
a:[0],e:"Without a mapping built at design time, the first business review's question (what is driving the change in handle time?) requires retrospective reconstruction instead of a live query. Discernment, the habit of judging output quality, feeds judgments back into evals and monitoring."},
{d:14,q:"Select the TWO tasks the course would assign to code-based evals rather than model-based evals.",
o:["Confirm that the extracted claim number matches the quantitative value in the source document, a check a function can make deterministically",
"Verify that all required sections (executive summary, methodology, findings, recommendations) are present, a presence check that needs no judgment",
"Assess whether the model's reasoning in a complex analysis is sound and complete, a question that a schema or regex can settle in milliseconds",
"Score how appropriately the system handles an emotionally charged customer complaint, a judgment that a deterministic function can apply reliably"],
a:[0, 1],e:"Behaviors with an unambiguous answer (valid JSON, length limits, exact-match against authoritative data, required sections present) go to code-based checks. Reasoning soundness, appropriateness in emotionally charged situations, tone and whether a summary captures the key points require interpretation, so they go to a calibrated model-based judge."},
{d:14,q:"A customer-service agent handles 50,000 requests a month with a stable 5,000-token system prompt, 300 input and 400 output tokens, a $800 monthly ceiling and a 3-second p95 target. Which lever does the course say did the most work in reaching both targets?",
o:["Turning prompt caching on, because the stable 5,000-token system prompt is the dominant input-cost driver and caching cuts that cost on every request",
"Moving to the most capable model tier, because a stronger model finishes tasks in fewer turns and so lowers both cost and latency at this volume",
"Raising the max_tokens cap, because more output headroom improves quality and quality improvements reduce retries, which lowers cost per resolved request",
"Switching to the smallest tier regardless of quality, because per-token price is the dominant cost driver and the latency target depends only on model size"],
a:[0],e:"In the course's calculator, the stable system prompt is repeated across all 50,000 requests, so caching it cuts the dominant input-cost driver. Most capable is not always safest, and a larger output cap adds cost without moving the targets. Figures in the course are representative; confirm current rates."},
{d:11,q:"A logistics company wants a system that reads 5,000 unstructured carrier emails a day, extracts delay reasons and new ETAs, and writes them to the order-management system, within 10 seconds and $1,000 a month. Which verdict and load-bearing constraint does the course give?",
o:["Feasible with constraints: accuracy on a transactional write is the limit, so add a code-based accuracy eval and a human gate on low-confidence output",
"Feasible as scoped: Haiku with caching handles the volume and the latency, and no capability needs a compensating control before the write to the system",
"Not feasible: 5,000 emails a day is too high a volume for any single deployment, so the scope must be cut to a few hundred before any build begins",
"Feasible with constraints: the limit is latency under ten seconds, so the design needs a fallback model on every call and nothing else to be added"],
a:[0],e:"The load-bearing constraint is extraction accuracy on a write to the system of record. A verdict alone is not enough; the constraint is what makes it defensible. Volume and latency are within reach, so the right answer is feasible with constraints: a code-based eval on extraction accuracy and a human review gate on low-confidence extractions before writing."},
{d:11,q:"A financial firm wants real-time trading recommendations from current market conditions plus its proprietary model, delivered in under 2 seconds. What does the course say about feasibility?",
o:["Not feasible as described: the live-state gap means a tool call to a market feed is needed, and its round trip must be validated inside 2 seconds first",
"Feasible as scoped: the model already knows current market conditions from training, so the proprietary model is the only integration the design needs",
"Feasible with constraints: add a human approval gate to each recommendation, which settles the knowledge limitation and the two-second budget together",
"Not feasible in any form: financial recommendations can never be produced by a language model, whatever data or tools the architecture provides"],
a:[0],e:"Real-time market data is live state the model cannot know, so it requires a tool call to a live feed. Whether that round trip fits the 2-second budget must be validated before any feasibility verdict can be issued. A human gate does not fix the knowledge gap or the latency budget."},
{d:11,q:"A firm wants an assistant that summarizes 10–40 page industry reports and drafts client briefings: 50 reports a week, briefings within 24 hours, and a $500 monthly ceiling. Which verdict does the course give?",
o:["Feasible as scoped: input fits the context window, volume, latency and cost are within range at the Sonnet tier, and no AI property is disqualifying",
"Not feasible: reports of up to 40 pages are too long for any context window, so the design must first split every report into separate sections",
"Feasible with constraints: a human review gate is required on every briefing, because language models cannot be trusted to summarize any long document",
"Feasible with constraints: the deployment needs the Opus tier and extended thinking for every report, because summarization is a reasoning-heavy task"],
a:[0],e:"Everything fits: 50 reports a week is low volume, a 24-hour deadline is loose, 10–40 pages fits the context window, and cost is within the ceiling at the Sonnet tier. Document the assumptions, because feasible-as-scoped verdicts become infeasible-with-constraints when assumptions change."},
{d:13,q:"In the course's diagram critique of a multi-tenant SaaS customer-service deployment, which TWO components are integration problems? Select two.",
o:["A shared API key used for all tenants, which prevents attributing rate-limit breaches or isolating tenants from one another",
"Claude Code used as the backend for the customer-facing chat product, an engineering entry point serving a non-engineering audience",
"A server-side authentication layer in front of the API, which verifies identity before the Claude call, and so is the sound pattern",
"Tenant data isolated per tenant at the storage layer, which keeps each tenant's records separate, and so is the sound pattern as drawn"],
a:[0, 1],e:"Problems: the shared API key (use per-tenant keys for attribution and isolation) and Claude Code as a chat backend (the entry point should follow the user and the work). The server-side authentication layer and per-tenant storage isolation are the sound parts of the design."},
{d:15,q:"In the same diagram, which TWO components are data-handling or authorization problems? Select two.",
o:["A capability check based on “I am a premium customer” in the user message, an unverified claim that the user controls and can fake",
"Claude's response passed to the downstream CRM with no logging at the integration layer, leaving that data path invisible to any audit",
"Server-side injection of the user's role into the system prompt, taken from the authentication layer, which is the sound pattern for identity",
"Account data kept in a per-tenant store that retrieval reads from, which keeps each tenant's lookups inside its own records and is sound"],
a:[0, 1],e:"Identity and entitlements must come from the server-side authentication layer, not from the user's own claim. An unlogged data path is invisible, so when something goes wrong there is no evidence to reconstruct what happened. The server-side role injection and per-tenant store are the sound patterns."},
{d:14,q:"Which scenario does the course's experiment-design exercise place at a high confidence requirement, calling for the most rigorous experiment and largest sample?",
o:["A prompt architecture change for a medical intake summarizer, where an error could delay treatment, so acting on a noisy result is costly",
"A minor wording change to a clarification message in a low-stakes FAQ chatbot, where a wrong call costs little and can be reversed quickly",
"A new classification category in a routing model expected to capture 30% of volume, where the large expected effect is easy to detect and verify",
"A Sonnet-to-Haiku switch on a simple formatting task, where a cost saving with unchanged quality can be confirmed on a standard evaluation set"],
a:[0],e:"Two axes set the experimental posture: expected effect size and how certain you must be before acting. Stakes drive the confidence requirement; a small expected effect at high confidence drives the largest minimum sample size. Large expected effects are easier to detect, and low-stakes reversible changes can accept lower confidence."},
{d:15,q:"A 600-person consulting firm wants a knowledge assistant over 12,000 SharePoint documents, some under client NDA, with Okta SSO. How should identity and confidential documents be handled?",
o:["Authenticate via Okta on the server, inject the verified role, and enforce document permissions in retrieval so only permitted excerpts enter context",
"Let consultants state their engagement in the chat, then retrieve from all 12,000 documents and ask the model to withhold anything covered by an NDA agreement",
"Index all documents into one shared store with a service account that reads everything, and filter NDA material from the answers by prompt instruction",
"Pass each consultant's full SharePoint profile and permissions list into the prompt, so the model can decide which excerpts that person is allowed to see"],
a:[0],e:"Identity belongs on the server, taken from the SSO layer, not from user input. Authorization must be enforced where the data is retrieved, so confidential excerpts a user may not see never enter the context window. Asking the model to withhold, or to decide from a pasted permission list, leaves the over-privileged path intact."},
{d:14,q:"For the firm's retrieval change, baseline task success is 70%, the goal is detecting a 5-point gain, and traffic is 800 requests a day. What does the course's method imply?",
o:["Compute sample size from effect size, baseline and confidence first, derive duration from traffic, and control input mix by document size and type",
"Run for a fixed two days, since 800 requests a day gives 1,600 sessions, which is always enough to detect a five-point change from a 70% baseline",
"Stop the test as soon as the treatment shows a gain of five points, since a result at the target threshold is already statistically significant evidence",
"Choose the primary metric after the test from whichever of cost, latency or success moved most, since a five-point gain could appear in any of them"],
a:[0],e:"Calculate the required sample size before the test, from the minimum detectable effect, the baseline and the confidence level; LLM output variance is higher than for deterministic systems, so the sample is larger. Traffic then sets duration. Control the input distribution (here, document size and type across the corpus), and never choose the primary metric after seeing results."},
{d:14,q:"Which alert design matches the course's anomaly-detection guidance for a production Claude system?",
o:["Threshold alerts on metrics that matter (cost above 150% of the 7-day average, p95 over the SLA), plus periodic distribution comparison to catch drift",
"Threshold alerts on average latency and daily error count only, because gradual drift in the model's outputs cannot be detected by any monitoring",
"Alerts on every metric at tight thresholds, so any movement triggers a review, since a quiet dashboard means nobody is checking the system closely",
"A weekly human review of five random transcripts, because automated alerts on cost and latency cannot be set without knowing the right values first"],
a:[0],e:"Set threshold alerts on the metrics that matter for the deployment; a cost spike over about 150% of the 7-day average or a p95 above the SLA deserves an alert. Model drift is harder to catch with thresholds and benefits from periodic comparison of output distributions."},
{d:14,q:"What does the course mean by Discernment when applied to a production system?",
o:["Judging each output as acceptable, needs revision or needs override, and feeding that judgment back into the evals and the ongoing monitoring",
"Choosing a model tier for each request at runtime by estimating its difficulty and routing it to the least expensive adequate model for the task",
"Describing the task precisely to the model, including scope, format and constraints, so that the prompt holds up beyond the demonstration stage",
"Deciding which tasks to delegate to Claude and which to people, by reversibility, stakes and accountability, before any architecture is drawn up"],
a:[0],e:"Discernment is one of the four AI Fluency competencies: judging the quality of what the model produced rather than accepting it at face value. Description (telling the model precisely what you want) and Delegation (deciding what Claude owns) are the other two named in the course. A team without Discernment watches metrics move without asking whether outputs were good."},
{d:13,q:"In an orchestrator-workers system, a subagent silently drops a unit and traces fragment across agents. Which mitigations does the course give?",
o:["Define recoverable (subagent) and unrecoverable (orchestrator) boundaries, share one trace ID across agents, and reconcile coverage so results equal units sent",
"Give each subagent its own independent trace and log store so failures stay isolated, and let the orchestrator summarize whatever results it receives",
"Retry the whole run from the start whenever any subagent fails, and treat the fluency of the final answer as the signal that every unit was covered",
"Run all subagents sequentially in one shared context so no unit can be dropped, and remove the orchestrator's synthesis step to simplify the tracing"],
a:[0],e:"Failure boundaries between orchestrators and subagents blur, traces fragment, and a dropped subagent can fail silently at synthesis. The mitigation is a clear recoverable-versus-unrecoverable boundary, a shared trace ID, and a coverage reconciliation step; see also the Module 1 fan-out case."},
{d:15,q:"A team says prompt data is safe because Anthropic does not retain conversation content by default. Why does the course still require a deliberate data-handling decision?",
o:["The request still crosses the wire, some retention carve-outs exist for certain model classes, and the partner's own application layer logs what it sends",
"Default non-retention applies only to the consumer apps, so API requests are always stored and the only control is to avoid sending any personal data",
"Non-retention means the model forgets earlier turns, so each request must repeat the full history, which is the only way sensitive fields can leak",
"Non-retention covers only text content, so images and files must be redacted, while text fields in the user message are safe to send as they are"],
a:[0],e:"The context window is not a data-governance boundary. Conversation content is not retained by default on the API, but the request is transmitted, specific retention carve-outs exist, and the application layer typically logs requests in plaintext. Decide which fields are necessary in the context window, and verify Anthropic's data-residency guidance against your regulatory requirements."},
{d:15,q:"A team finishes an integration on a convenient route, and the next legal review finds the route does not meet the governing constraint. What does the course say this costs?",
o:["The time already invested plus a new architecture from scratch, which is why compliance constraints are applied first and eliminate routes before design begins",
"A configuration change at the delivery route only, since the entry point and the prompts carry over unchanged to any other route that does pass review",
"A short delay while the model tier is swapped, because compliance reviews judge the model's capability and not the route that the traffic takes to it",
"Nothing material, because the review can approve a documented exception once the team logs each affected request and keeps the logs for the audit period"],
a:[0],e:"Compliance sits first because it eliminates routes and entry points before any other decision. Finding the failure at a legal or security review means the cost of redesign is the time already invested plus a new architecture from scratch."},
{d:15,q:"A team plans to add logging after the first incident, since nothing has gone wrong yet. What does the course say about that plan?",
o:["An unlogged data path is invisible, so there is no evidence to reconstruct an incident, and building observability afterward always costs more than building it first",
"It is reasonable for low-risk paths, because logs only matter for regulated data and the first incident will show exactly which paths were worth instrumenting",
"It is acceptable if the model is evaluated before launch, since a passing evaluation shows what the logs would otherwise have been needed to reconstruct later",
"It works if infrastructure logs are already on, because errors and timeouts capture the failure cases that a subtly wrong model response would produce in use"],
a:[0],e:"Observability and audit logging answer what you need to reconstruct after an incident. An unlogged data path is invisible; when something goes wrong there, no evidence exists to reconstruct what happened, and the cost of building it after the first incident is always higher than before."},
{d:15,q:"Which pairing of an integration layer with what breaks when it is decided wrongly matches the course?",
o:["Identity and SSO: user identity passed as a raw field in the user message is manipulable, while server-side injection removes that risk",
"Authorization and policy: a model that is too small applies access rules unreliably, so the fix is moving access decisions to a larger model tier",
"Data handling and PII: an overlong system prompt is the main risk, because sensitive fields are exposed only through the prompt and never the message",
"Compliance: a route that fails review can be corrected late with a settings change, because regulated constraints apply after the design is complete"],
a:[0],e:"Each layer has its own failure. Identity passed in the user message is manipulable, so inject it server-side. Authorization must be enforced at the system layer, PII in any part of the request reaches logs in plaintext, and compliance failures force a redesign from scratch."},
{d:14,q:"A regional insurer's Claude system extracts claim fields, summarizes the narrative for an adjuster, and flags possible fraud, under a few-second limit, a per-claim cost ceiling, no cross-claimant leakage, and no auto-deny. For its evaluation framework: Accuracy: field extraction (claimant, policy number, loss amount, date of loss). Which metric, grading method and reason fit best?",
o:["Field-level exact match against a labeled golden set, graded by code, because each field has one unambiguous correct value that needs no interpretation",
"An overall quality score from an LLM judge reading each extraction, because a judge can weigh partial matches the way an adjuster would in practice",
"An adjuster satisfaction rating from human review of every extraction, because people know best whether a policy number or loss amount looks right",
"Field completeness rate graded by code, counting populated fields without comparing values to the golden set, because that is cheap to run on every release"],
a:[0],e:"Extraction of specific values has an unambiguous right answer, so code-based exact match against a golden set is the cheapest and most reliable grader. A judge or human is reserved for behavior that needs interpretation. Completeness counts fields without checking that they are right."},
{d:14,q:"A regional insurer's Claude system extracts claim fields, summarizes the narrative for an adjuster, and flags possible fraud, under a few-second limit, a per-claim cost ceiling, no cross-claimant leakage, and no auto-deny. For its evaluation framework: Latency: response time against the “few seconds” requirement. Which metric, grading method and reason fit best?",
o:["End-to-end p95 response time per claim, measured by code in the test harness and in production, because timing is a number and the tail breaks the limit",
"Average model-call time only, measured by code, because the model is the one component the team controls and the rest of the pipeline is out of scope",
"An LLM judge's rating of how responsive each reply feels, because perceived speed depends on the content and a judge can read the content of a reply",
"Adjuster survey scores on perceived speed, collected weekly, because the people who use the system are the best judges of whether it feels fast enough"],
a:[0],e:"Latency p95 is a number, and the check is whether it falls below the target; no judgment is involved. Use the tail (p95), not the average, because slow cases break the limit. Judges and surveys add interpretation to a quantity that is already a number."},
{d:14,q:"A regional insurer's Claude system extracts claim fields, summarizes the narrative for an adjuster, and flags possible fraud, under a few-second limit, a per-claim cost ceiling, no cross-claimant leakage, and no auto-deny. For its evaluation framework: Safety: summary faithfulness and no auto-deny. Which grading methods fit the two behaviors?",
o:["Faithfulness by an LLM judge calibrated against human-graded samples; no auto-deny by a code check that no output or action ever sets a denial",
"Faithfulness by code-based string overlap with the narrative; no auto-deny by an LLM judge that reads each flag and decides whether it implies a denial",
"Both by human review of every summary and every flag, because safety behaviors should only ever be graded by a person and never by an automated check",
"Both by one LLM judge, because a judge covers every behavior and a single grader keeps the framework simple to run, report and maintain after launch"],
a:[0],e:"Two methods are required. The deny action is binary (a denial was issued or it was not), so use a code-based eval. Faithfulness, whether the narrative represents the source claim without fabrication, is interpretive and a function cannot encode it, so use an LLM judge. Human review is the last resort."},
{d:14,q:"A regional insurer's Claude system extracts claim fields, summarizes the narrative for an adjuster, and flags possible fraud, under a few-second limit, a per-claim cost ceiling, no cross-claimant leakage, and no auto-deny. For its evaluation framework: Security: no cross-claimant data leakage. Which metric, grading method and reason fit best?",
o:["A code scan of each summary for identifiers from any input claim other than the one processed, because leakage is a deterministic check with no judgment needed",
"An LLM judge asked whether each summary seems to mention anyone else, because a judge can recognize indirect references that a text search would miss",
"A human spot-check of one percent of summaries each month, because leakage is rare and a small sample is enough to show whether the rate is rising",
"A system-prompt instruction not to mention other claimants, verified on a sample by a judge, because the instruction alone prevents the leak at the source"],
a:[0],e:"Leakage is checked deterministically by scanning each summary for identifiers that appear in any input claim other than the one being processed; no interpretation is needed. A prompt instruction is not a control, and samples miss rare events."},
{d:14,q:"A regional insurer's Claude system extracts claim fields, summarizes the narrative for an adjuster, and flags possible fraud, under a few-second limit, a per-claim cost ceiling, no cross-claimant leakage, and no auto-deny. For its evaluation framework: Cost: per-claim spend against the defined ceiling. Which metric, grading method and reason fit best?",
o:["Cost per claim computed by code from input tokens, output tokens, model tier and caching, checked against the ceiling, because cost is a numeric value",
"The monthly invoice divided by claims processed, reviewed by finance each quarter, because the invoice is the authoritative record of what was spent",
"Estimated cost from average prompt length at design time, checked once before launch, because claim sizes vary little from one claim to the next one",
"A weekly human look at the billing dashboard to decide whether spend feels acceptable, because acceptable cost is a business judgment and not a metric"],
a:[0],e:"Cost is a numeric value derived from input tokens, output tokens, model tier and whether prompt caching applies; the check is whether it exceeds the ceiling. A quarterly invoice or one-off estimate finds overruns too late."}
  ]
});
